WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.290
THE STATE OF WHATSAPP:
WhatsApp is one of the largest and most ubiquitous

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.980
social media platforms in the world.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:11.300
With 2.7 billion monthly active users and
over 100 billion messages being sent every

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.740
single day, it’s no question that WhatsApp
is extremely successful from a market share

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.710
perspective, but the same cannot be said from
a financial perspective.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:27.060
It’s been nearly 10 years since Facebook
bought WhatsApp but their revenue doesn’t

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.460
quite match up with their popularity.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.750
In fact, it’s only now that WhatsApp is
even crossing the billion dollar mark in terms

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:34.760
of revenue.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:40.050
Put another way, WhatApp only makes about
40 cents per user per year.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.500
For perspective, YouTube has a similar user
count of 2.7 billion monthly active users,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.890
but they pull in nearly $30 billion in revenue
per year or over $10 per user per year.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.090
Even TikTok, a platform that’s not known
to have the most monetizable of demographics

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.180
is expected to pull in $13 billion this year.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.960
But all of that is nothing in comparison to
the true king of social media monetization

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.590
and WhatsApp’s younger brother: Instagram.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:13.659
In 2023, Instagram is expected to pull in
$50 billion or over $20 per user.

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:18.940
For those of you who have haven’t been keeping
track, that’s over 50 times more per user

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.159
per year that WhatsApp, and that’s not too
surprising.

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:29.250
The reality is that it’s quite hard to monetize
a free to use messaging platform, and believe

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.399
me, it’s not due to a lack of effort.

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:37.320
Over the years, Facebook has tried strategy
after strategy but all of them end up falling

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.250
flat because of the tricky game their trying
to play.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.900
They don’t want to turn away users by making
the platform paid or by showing ads, but at

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.729
the same time, the platform does eventually
have to make money one way or another.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:54.549
And this has pressure has been cranked up
to the max with the high interest and high

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:58.560
inflation environment where investors expect
companies to take a step back from growth

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.880
and really focus in on profitability.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.469
So, what really is the long term financial
play with WhatsApp and will WhatsApp ever

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:09.360
be a lucrative business?

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.230
A LIFELONG STRUGGLE:
Taking a look back, WhatsApp’s struggle

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.060
to monetize can be traced all the way back
to day one in 2009.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.960
From the very beginning, WhatsApp was kept
afloat by investors.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.430
First, it was the founder’s friends from
Yahoo who agreed to invest $250,000 to get

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.430
the company going.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.540
Then, it was $8 million from Sequoia and another
$50 million from Sequoia.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:41.180
The only way that WhatsApp made money was
by charging users a dollar per year but that

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.830
was mainly just to cover the cost of verification
texts, so the actual cost of running the platform

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.750
and paying engineers had to come out of pocket.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.360
WhatsApp was likely hoping that a lot of these
financial concerns could be addressed by scale

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.670
but scale only made things worse.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:02.180
In fact, by the time that Facebook got involved,
WhatsApp had already reached 400 million active

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.239
users.

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:08.819
In the first half of 2014, however, these
400 million users only translated to a mere

00:03:08.819 --> 00:03:15.720
$15.9 million worth of revenue while the bottom
line stood at, wait for it, -$232.5 million.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:23.790
In other words, WhatsApp was burning nearly
half a billion per year with 400 million users.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.040
If these losses were to scale to their current
size, WhatsApp would be burning over $3 billion

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.040
per year.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.150
Clearly, the only way to make such a business
viable was to get the help of a tech giant

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.709
like Google or Facebook that could eat up
the losses, and that’s why WhatsApp would

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:40.390
eventually be sold to Facebook.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:44.750
With the help of Facebook, you would think
that WhatsApp’s financial situation would

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.880
get bette but the exact opposite actually
happened.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.930
WhatsApp no longer had to worry about staying
afloat and operationAtal but this simultaneously

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.120
made revenue generation even less of a concern.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.970
In fact, Facebook not only neglected generating
more revenue with WhatsApp, but they straight

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.860
up undid revenue generation efforts that were
already in place.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:17.759
FACEBOOK MAKES THINGS WORSE:
Kicking of 2016, Facebook would drop WhatsApp’s

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:22.070
annual $1 fee while also promising to stay
ad-free.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:23.150
Why you ask?

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.480
Well, the official reasoning is that WhatsApp
wanted users who didn’t have a debit or

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.009
credit card to be able to stay in contact
with their family.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:35.539
Reading between the lines though, WhatsApp
basically saw the $1 fee as a barrier to gaining

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:41.500
market share and utter market dominance, so
they dropped the fee.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.760
This move was rather questionable though for
a few reasons.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:51.350
First of all, it’s only $1 per year, and
I think that virtually everyone in the world

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:56.880
would agree that that’s a reasonable fee
for a messaging platform on a daily basis.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.310
Second of all, avoiding the annual fee was
already quite easy, so users who really didn’t

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.310
want to or couldn’t pay didn’t have to.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:10.560
And third of all, WhatsApp had already brought
the cost of messaging by hundreds of fold.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.889
In fact, Forbes estimates that between 2012
and 2018, free messaging services like WhatsApp

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:23.340
and Skype had lost the telecommunications
industry a total of $386 billion.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.949
At the time, such services had a combined
user count of about 2 billion.

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:34.600
So, according to this estimate, these services
were saving users on average $32 per year.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.889
At that rate, even charging $10 per user per
year would’ve been reasonable much less

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:44.960
$1 per year and this would’ve completely
changed the trajectory of the business.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:50.280
Even at $1 per year, WhatsApp would’ve crossed
the billion mark in terms of revenue way back

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.850
in 2015.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.840
And if they eventually increment this to $2
per year and then $3 per year, it’s quite

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:01.789
possible that WhatsApp would’ve been pulling
in north of $10 billion per year in revenue

00:06:01.789 --> 00:06:02.789
today.

00:06:02.789 --> 00:06:06.069
But, Facebook would end up choosing growth
over everything else.

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:10.150
It’s possible that Facebook was thinking
that they could user data collection as a

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:15.120
way to monetize WhatsApp but this led to a
wholenother slew of issues.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.130
Kicking it off in May of 2017, the European
commission would fine WhatsApp $122 million.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:26.150
It turns out that WhatsApp was sharing user
data such as phone numbers with Facebook in

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.850
order to retarget these users with Facebok
ads.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.290
The fine, however, was the least of Facebook’s
worries.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:37.430
WhatsApp’s founders started to fear that
this was the new direction of WhatsApp, and

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.360
as a result, they would both quit within the
next year, leaving over a billion dollars

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:42.720
on the table to do so.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.410
One of the founders would actually go on to
create a rival non profit messaging platform

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.940
and that’s what turned out to be Signal.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.990
Atleast with the founders gone though, Facebook
could roll out shady monetization strategies

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:59.810
with less resistance or atleast that’s what
they thought until they got caught up in an

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.479
even bigger situation.

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:06.190
In 2018, Facebook would get caught up with
the Cambridge Analytica scandal, the largest

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.350
social media scandal of all time.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:12.310
If you’re not familiar with the scandal,
Facebook was basically involved in a shady

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:17.349
data collection effort that may have influenced
the 2016 presidential election.

00:07:17.349 --> 00:07:22.340
Facebook would end up having to pay a $5 billion
fine, the largest privacy related fine in

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.139
history by a full magnitude.

00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:30.660
While Facebook was able to afford the fine,
their public reputation was permanently damaged.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.030
And the last thing they wanted to do with
WhatsApp was create some sort of movement

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.230
against themselves by implementing some sort
of shady monetization strategy.

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:44.159
So, Facebook would lay low when it came to
WhatsApp monetization over the next few years

00:07:44.159 --> 00:07:48.370
leading us to their current monetization crisis.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:56.030
A DEAD END:
Facebook would eventually come out with a

00:07:56.030 --> 00:08:01.970
rather creative avenue to monetize users indirectly
while also remaining ethical, and that avenue

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.370
was WhatsApp business.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:08.590
WhatsApp business launched at the beginning
of 2018 as a way for businesses to leverage

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:11.090
the infrastructure of WhatsApp.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:15.490
The way it works is that businesses can use
the WhatsApp business app or Whatsapp’s

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:19.620
business APIs in order to communicate with
customers on a familiar platform.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.460
The first 1000 conversations every month are
free, but after that, businesses have to pay

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.430
for every conversation.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:31.270
This is primarily how WhatsApp has been generating
revenue over the past couple of years, and

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:36.209
Zuckerberg is optimistic that WhatsApp business
is the future of WhatsApp, and it very well

00:08:36.209 --> 00:08:37.440
could be.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.599
WhatsApp Business could be the feature that
takes WhatsApp from what is at best a break

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:45.690
even business and turn it into a lucrative
busines.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:51.589
But with that being said, this still neglects
monetizing the bread and butter of WhatsApp:

00:08:51.589 --> 00:08:57.649
peer to peer communication, and to be honest,
I don’t think that will change.

00:08:57.649 --> 00:09:02.950
The reality is that if Facebook wants to continue
growing WhatsApp’s user base, they cannot

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.450
make any sort of strong monetization effort.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:06.450
Why?

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:10.500
Well, because the main competition for WhatApp
is now completely different.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.240
When WhatsApp launched, the main competition
was regular old SMS texting and expensive

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:16.300
phone plans.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.740
So, when WhatsApp offered a cheap alternative,
people ate it up.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:25.769
Today, WhatsApp’s main competition is no
longer phone plans, their main competition

00:09:25.769 --> 00:09:28.420
is Apple and more specifically iMessage.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:33.970
Now, hearing that, you might be saying that
iPhone users are only a fraction of the global

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:37.770
smartphone market and that Android is the
dominant player.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:43.500
But here’s the thing, western iPhone users
are the only people who don’t yet already

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.250
use WhatsApp.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:51.579
Everywhere else is already literally 100%
saturated by WhatsApp and I’m not even exaggerating.

00:09:51.579 --> 00:09:56.260
In Brazil, WhatsApp’s market share is 98.9%.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:00.090
In India, their market share is 97.1%.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:05.820
In Italy, it’s 97% and it’s the same way
basically everywhere else other than a few

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:06.950
exceptions.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:11.680
These exceptions include Australia, Sweden,
the United States, Canada, and France where

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.240
WhatsApp’s marketshare hovers in the 30
to 40% range.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.390
You know what else these countries have in
common?

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.040
Well, theses are the few countries around
the world where Apple dominates.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:27.149
In fact, in the US, Apple straight up controls
the majority with 56% market share.

00:10:27.149 --> 00:10:33.029
And this lines up almost exactly with WhatsApp’s
41% market share within the US.

00:10:33.029 --> 00:10:37.899
And here’s the thing, Apple never needs
iMessage to be profitable.

00:10:37.899 --> 00:10:42.400
I’m not even sure how you would measure
the profitability of iMessage.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.880
From their perspective, iMessage is simply
one facet that sells their actual product

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.860
which is the iPhone experience.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.610
So, they have 0 ulterior motives when it comes
to iMessage.

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:58.560
It’s in their best interest to keep iMessage
as great as possible for free, and that’s

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.380
what WhatsApp is having to go up against.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.470
When it comes to privacy centric iPhone users,
WhatsApp’s association with Facebook itself

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.550
takes them down a peg.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:12.250
If you combine this with some sort of pay
wall or shady monetization strategy, you basically

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.240
give up all chances of converting these customers.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.330
But let’s even forget about acquiring new
users.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:22.360
With Android bleeding market share globally,
WhatsApp has to make sure that they don’t

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:28.800
end up bleeding users to iMessage which all
leaves WhatsApp in a rather sticky situation.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.350
They’ve sacrificed revenue and profit over
the past decade just so that they can maximize

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.700
market share and eventually monetize.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:39.899
But, now that they’re on the other side,
it turns out that they have to continue this

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:42.730
arrangement if they want to protect their
market share.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:46.890
And instead of monetizing that main consumer
use of their platform, they’re having to

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:50.070
monetize business use of their platform.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:54.839
But hey, maybe that’s not such a bad thing.

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:59.850
Unlike Facebook as a whole which very much
got bloated, WhatsApp has always been a pretty

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:04.060
lean business probably because WhatsApp never
generated all that much revenue.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:08.829
In fact, WhatsApp only needed 50 engineers
to reach 900 million users.

00:12:08.829 --> 00:12:14.490
So, it’s likely that the WhatsApp team is
still only in the low triple digits.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:19.800
So, WhatsApp doesn't need to make billions
and billions of dollars to remain self sustainable

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.420
or worth pursuing.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:27.870
WhatsApp may never become a cash cow for Meta,
but that doesn’t stop WhatsApp from carrying

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:33.820
the Meta name, keeping the company relevant,
and earning them some good will which is definitely

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:34.840
valuable.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:40.130
Clearly, Facebook has yet to figure out how
to monetize WhatsApp’s massive user base,

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:45.480
but something that they have figured out is
how to monetize their $61 billion cash reserves

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.590
with the help of high yielding treasury bills
and bonds.

